    "q-143110": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143110",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143110-a",
