    "q-143107": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143107",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **squared distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *squared distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
