    },
    "q-144820": {
      "dom_id": "step-q144820",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
