    "q-143134": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143134",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The area of a square increases at a rate proportional to the cube of its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **cube of its perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the cube of its perimeter*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k(4s)^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =k(4s)^{3} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =64k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64k{s}^{3}}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =32k{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=32k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
