    "q-80491": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80491",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "The volume of a cube decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=6{s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6k{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6k{s}^{2}}{3{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
