    "q-143125": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143125",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "The volume of a cube increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the length of its edge. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the edge length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **length of its edge**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional to the length of its edge.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{3{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{3}}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
