    "q-66523": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66523",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$",
